-- title:
--   M₂(mathbb Zₚ) as a crossed product of W(mathbb F_{p²})
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime. Write $W = \mathrm{WittVector}\ p\ (\mathrm{GaloisField}\ p\ 2)$ for the ring of Witt vectors of the field with $p^2$ elements, and let $\mathrm{WittVector.frobenius}$ be its Witt-vector Frobenius endomorphism. The assertion is that there exist a ring homomorphism $r \colon W \to M_2(\mathbb Z_p)$ into the ring of $2\times 2$ matrices over the $p$-adic integers, and a matrix $S \in M_2(\mathbb Z_p)$, such that four conditions hold simultaneously: $S \cdot S = 1$; for every $a \in W$ one has $S \cdot r(a) = r(\mathrm{frobenius}\ a)\cdot S$; for all $a, c \in W$, if $r(a) + r(c)\cdot S = 0$ then $a = 0$ and $c = 0$; and every matrix $M \in M_2(\mathbb Z_p)$ can be written as $M = r(a) + r(c)\cdot S$ for some $a, c \in W$. Since $r$ is additive, the last two clauses together say that $(a,c) \mapsto r(a) + r(c)S$ is a bijection $W \times W \to M_2(\mathbb Z_p)$, so that $M_2(\mathbb Z_p) = r(W) \oplus r(W)S$ as $r(W)$-modules.
--
--   This exhibits $M_2(\mathbb Z_p)$ as the trivial crossed product of the unramified quadratic extension $\mathbb Z_{p^2} = W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$ of $\mathbb Z_p$ with the Galois group generated by Frobenius, the classical identification of the twisted group ring $\mathbb Z_{p^2}\langle\sigma\rangle$ with $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb Z_p}(\mathbb Z_{p^2})$. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd part of the development, in the analysis of special formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules, where the existence of such an $r$ and $S$ supplies the required embedding of $W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$ and the Frobenius-twisting involution; the proof invokes the bijectivity of $a \mapsto \sum_i \mathrm{map}(\mathrm{algebraMap})(a_i)\cdot \mathrm{teichmuller}(b_i)$ attached to a finite basis $b$ of a perfect characteristic-$p$ extension, which gives the free $W(\mathbb F_p)$-module structure of $W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$ in the Teichmüller basis.
